course: dictionary
lesson: 8458
title: Sake: one word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Sake is a noun, but you will mostly meet it in short phrases. Read the words around it to find out which meaning it has. «She will never laugh at you again,» says Suleiman, and for the Butterfly's sake, not to show off, he turns his ring. Suleiman acts for the Butterfly's good, so for someone's sake means to help them or for their good.
explain: For the sake of something means because of it, or to get it. In a safety talk you might hear, «For safety's sake, wear a helmet.» The clue is safety: the helmet is worn because of safety. Ask yourself: what is the reason for doing this?
explain: Some phrases show feelings. «For goodness' sake!» and «For Pete's sake» show that the speaker is annoyed or impatient. Pete's sake is a mild way of saying something stronger, so it is a gentle phrase. When you hear sake after for and the speaker sounds cross, that is your clue.
fact: Sake has several meanings, so the words around it tell you which one is meant.
fact: «For someone's sake» means to help them or for their good.
fact: «For goodness' sake» and «for Pete's sake» are mild ways of showing you are annoyed or impatient.
fact: Sake (said SAH-kay) is also a Japanese alcoholic drink made from rice, so it is not for children.
fact: Some phrases, such as «for God's sake», are rude in some homes and schools, so avoid them.
exercise: «She will never laugh at you again,» says Suleiman, and for the Butterfly's sake, he turns his ring. What does «for the Butterfly's sake» mean? | To make the Butterfly cross | *To help her or for her good | To show off to everyone | To win a prize from her
exercise: A safety sign says, «For safety's sake, wear a helmet.» What does «for safety's sake» mean? | To get a free helmet | For fun and games | Because the helmet is pretty | *Because of safety, to keep you safe
exercise: Someone shouts, «For goodness' sake, hurry up!» How does the speaker feel? | *Annoyed and impatient | Sleepy and bored | Very happy and calm | Curious and excited
test: «For Pete's sake» is a mild way of saying something stronger, showing annoyance. Which stronger phrase does it stand in for? | Thank you very much | Please | Good morning | *For God's sake
test: Sake, said SAH-kay, is a Japanese drink. What is it made from? | Tea | *Rice | Apples | Milk
exam: The phrase «for old times' sake» means what? | Without any reason at all | *Because of good times shared in the past | To cook a special meal | To win a race
